# Angular XSS: DomSanitizer.bypassSecurityTrust*() disables Angular's built-in
# output sanitisation. Passing anything user-influenced through it re-opens the
# XSS hole Angular closes by default (CWE-79).
#
# Found via OWASP Juice Shop's own author markers (2026-09-26):
#   frontend/src/app/last-login-ip/last-login-ip.component.ts:40
#   this.sanitizer.bypassSecurityTrustHtml(`<small>${payload.data.lastLoginIp}</small>`)
#   (+ search-result.component, data-export.component — all vuln-line tagged)
# keur had no Angular XSS rule (SEC-041 is generic server injection).
#
# Match any bypassSecurityTrust{Html,Script,Style,Url,ResourceUrl} call. Exclude
# test/spec files (they mock the sanitiser) so the rule fires on real components.
id: KEUR-XSS-001
ilities: [integrity]
maturity: 2
title: Angular XSS — DomSanitizer.bypassSecurityTrust* disables output sanitisation
category: xss
severity: error
engine: pattern
action: warn
target:
  extensions: .ts .js
  exclude_paths: .spec. .test. __tests__/ /test/ node_modules/
patterns:
  - regex: \bbypassSecurityTrust(?:Html|Script|Style|Url|ResourceUrl)\s*\(
    message: DomSanitizer.bypassSecurityTrust*() disables Angular's built-in XSS sanitisation (CWE-79). If any part of the value is user-influenced this re-opens the XSS hole Angular closes by default. Bind trusted values only, or sanitise explicitly.
confidence: 0.7
fix: Do not bypass the sanitiser for user-influenced data. Bind the raw value (Angular escapes it), or if HTML is genuinely required, sanitise it first (DOMPurify) and bypass only the sanitised result.
